---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../components/CodeBlock.astro";

const vanilla = `import { bindHotkey } from "@skryensya/vanilla/hotkey";

// 'mod' is ⌘ on Mac and Ctrl on the rest, a single spec, the
// dos plataformas.
const off = bindHotkey("mod+k", () => palette.showModal());

// returns its own cleanliness, like every enhancer
off();`;

const react = `import { useHotkey } from "@skryensya/react/hotkey";

function Palette() {
  const [open, setOpen] = useState(false);
  useHotkey("mod+k", () => setOpen((v) => !v));
  // …
}`;

const specs = `"mod+k"        // ⌘K on Mac, Ctrl+K on the rest
"mod+shift+p"  // exact combination: DOES NOT fire with "mod+p"
"shift+/"      // "?" for help
"escape"       // a single key, no modifier
"g > i"        // a sequence: g, then i`;

const format = `import { formatHotkey } from "@skryensya/core/hotkey";

formatHotkey("mod+k", true);   // "⌘K"   (Mac)
formatHotkey("mod+k", false);  // "Ctrl+K"
// the badge next to the search engine above is filled so`;
---

<Base
  title="Hotkey"
  description="The keyboard shortcut primitive: a framework-agnostic matcher, an imperative bindHotkey, and a React useHotkey."
  documentLayout="grid"
>
  <h1>Hotkey</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A keyboard shortcut is behavior without an equivalent on the platform, <code>accesskey</code> exists but the browser hides it behind its own chords and never gives a ⌘K type trigger. So the system ships it, built on <a href="https://zagjs.com/utilities/hotkeys"><code>@zag-js/hotkeys</code></a>: parsing, matching, formatting and binding live in <code>@skryensya/core</code>, and <code>@skryensya/vanilla</code> and <code>@skryensya/react</code> expose the same binding as a function and as a hook.
  </p>
  <p>
    Try it now: press <kbd class="sk-kbd" data-sk-command-palette-hint>⌘K</kbd>, the finder in this documentation is <a href="/components/command-palette">CommandPalette</a>, the component that consumes this primitive. The <a href="/components/kbd"><code>Kbd</code></a> also has a state that reacts to the keyboard with this same <code>matchesHotkey</code>; It is seen on your page.
  </p>

  <h2>Why <code>mod</code> is worth it</h2>
  <p>
    It is why this deserves a primitive and not a loose <code>event.metaKey</code>: <code>mod</code> is ⌘ on a Mac and Ctrl everywhere else, so a single spec, <code>"mod+k"</code>, is the correct chord on both without the writer ever branchingper platform. The core takes that data as a boolean instead of sniffing <code>navigator</code>, so it remains pure and testable; The bindings detect it and pass it on.
  </p>

  <h2>Vanilla</h2>
  <p>
    <code>bindHotkey(spec, handler)</code> registers the shortcut on its own Zag hotkey store and returns its own cleanup, the same way every system enhancer returns.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={vanilla} label="JavaScript" />

  <h2>React</h2>
  <p>
    <code>useHotkey(spec, handler)</code> is the same function as a hook: it calls the core's <code>bindHotkey</code> (never the vanilla package, the two bindings are siblings on the core), passes the handler through a ref so as not to re-bind on each render, and the effect's cleanup unbinds it.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={react} label="React" />

  <h2>The spec</h2>
  <p>
    Modifiers first, then the key, separated by <code>+</code>; <code>&gt;</code> separates the steps of a sequence. The chord is <strong>exact</strong>: <code>"mod+k"</code> does not trigger with <code>mod+shift+k</code>, so that a shortcut does not silently swallow a superset that the app wanted for something else.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={specs} label="specs" lang="ts" />

  <h2>Showing it</h2>
  <p>
    A shortcut is only discovered if it is seen. <code>formatHotkey</code> writes it as the operating system writes it, glyphs on Mac, words on the rest, for the badge next to a search engine or in a menu.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={format} label="JavaScript" />

  <h2>What it does for you, and what it does not</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>It does not trigger a single letter while you type. </strong> A <code>"/"</code> within an input is text, not a command, it is deleted. But a chord with modifier (⌘K) <em>always triggers</em>, because it has to reach you from within the search engine it opens. It can be forced with <code>enableWhileTyping</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Stops the default browser. </strong> A ⌘S that also saves the page is a bug, so it calls <code>preventDefault()</code> by default.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>It handles sequences</strong> (<code>"g &gt; i"</code>: <code>g</code>, then <code>i</code> within a second), but not scopes per widget: it is a spec and a handler. The rest is put together by whoever consumes it, like the search engine above.
    </li>
  </ul>
</Base>
